SUMMARY:Florida Conversations: An Evening with Mac Stone
LOCATION:801 Water Street Tampa \, FL 33602 United States
DESCRIPTION:Join the Tampa Bay History Center for an evening with award-wi
 nning National Geographic photographer Mac Stone (https:www.macstonephoto.
 comabout-1) as he shares his work documenting the last old-growth cypress 
 swamps of the American South. Drawing from more than a decade spent photog
 raphing ancient forests in North Carolina\, South Carolina\, and Florida\,
  Stone will share the stories and images behind his new book\,  Cypress: T
 he Last Old-Growth Swamps . From ghost orchids blooming high in ancient cy
 press trees to panthers moving through the wetlands below\, his photograph
 s reveal ecosystems that have endured for centuries and are increasingly r
 are. Through stunning photography and stories from the field\, Stone explo
 res these remarkable landscapes as living archives of deep time and consid
 ers what their survival means for the future of the South. Tickets are 5 a
 nd free for History Center members. Florida Conversations is supported by 
 the University of South Florida Endowment\, (https:giving.usf.eduhowendowm
 ents) USF Libraries (https:lib.usf.edu) and a generous gift from Shelley B
 lood. This program is presented in person in TECO Hall at the Tampa Bay Hi
 story Center and virtually through Zoom. A recording will be posted on You
 Tube after the event. About the speaker: Mac Stone is an award-winning Nat
 ional Geographic (https:www.nationalgeographic.com) photographer\, conserv
 ationist\, producer\, writer and naturalist whose work explores the relati
 onship between people and the wild landscapes of the American South. A tra
 ined biologist\, he uses photography\, science and storytelling to documen
 t wetlands\, wildlife and the ecological systems that connect the region. 
 He is the author of Everglades: Americas Wetland\, American Amazon: Ancien
 t Forests and Living Waters of the Wild Southeastern U.S. and Cypress: The
  Last Old-Growth Swamps. He worked as a producer and cinematographer on th
 e American Amazon IMAX film and PBS NATURE series. Stone also serves as ex
 ecutive director of Naturaland Trust\, where he advances land conservation
  and restoration across the Southeast. \n\nPermalink: https://www.tampa.go
 v/node/196201
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.tampa.gov/node/196201
END:VEVENT
